<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" dtd-version="1.3" xml:lang="en" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Korean J Radiol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Korean J Radiol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">KJR</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Korean Journal of Radiology</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1229-6929</issn><issn pub-type="epub">2005-8330</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Korean Society of Radiology</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41199129</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12683755</article-id>
<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3348/kjr.2025.0947</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Thoracic Imaging</subject><subj-group subj-group-type="subheading"><subject>Original Article</subject></subj-group></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Impact of Increased Chest CT Utilization on the Diagnosis of Pneumonia in Older Adults: A Population-Based Study of 930,654 Individuals</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid" authenticated="true">https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3697-5542</contrib-id><name><surname>Hwang</surname><given-names>Eui Jin</given-names></name><xref rid="A1" ref-type="aff"/></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="A1">Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, <country>Republic of Korea</country>.</aff><author-notes><corresp>Corresponding author: Eui Jin Hwang, MD, PhD, Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, 101 Daehak-ro, Jongno-gu, Seoul 03080, Republic of Korea. <email>ken921004@hotmail.com</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>12</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>06</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>26</volume><issue>12</issue><fpage>1178</fpage><lpage>1188</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>11</day><month>11</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>21</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>02</day><month>9</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2025 The Korean Society of Radiology</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><copyright-holder>The Korean Society of Radiology</copyright-holder><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution Non-Commercial License (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0</ext-link>) which permits unrestricted non-commercial use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract><sec><title>Objective</title><p>The trends in chest computed tomography (CT) utilization among patients with pneumonia and its association with pneumonia incidence and mortality remain unclear. This study aimed to investigate these trends and their associations in older adults.</p></sec><sec><title>Materials and Methods</title><p>We conducted a retrospective analysis of a Korean population aged 61&#x02013;81 years at each calendar year between 2009 and 2018, using data from the government-provided National Health Insurance claims database (annual cohort size: 511,931&#x02013;725,843 individuals). For each calendar year, we evaluated population-level, age- and sex-standardized pneumonia incidence and mortality rates; age- and sex-standardized frequency of chest CT acquisition, and 30-day mortality among patients with pneumonia. Pneumonia severity was categorized based on hospitalization and use of supplemental oxygen therapy. Incidence and mortality rates of pneumonia with and without chest CT were also evaluated for each severity subtype.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p>The age- and sex-standardized incidence rate of pneumonia increased from 27.7 to 29.4 per 1,000 person-years between 2009 and 2018. Incidence rate of pneumonia with chest CT increased from 3.7 to 5.9 per 1,000 person-years, whereas incidence rate of pneumonia without chest CT remained stable (24.1 to 23.4 per 1,000 person-years). The frequency of chest CT acquisition among patients with pneumonia rose from 13.4% to 20.4%, regardless of severity. Over the same period, the age- and sex-standardized pneumonia mortality rate decreased from 51.9 to 44.2 per 100,000 person-years, and 30-day mortality among patients with pneumonia declined from 2.1% to 1.7%, regardless of severity.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion</title><p>Chest CT acquisition among older Korean patients with pneumonia increased steadily between 2009 and 2018. The population-level pneumonia incidence also increased, mainly in pneumonia diagnosed with chest CT acquisition. Further research is needed to assess the potential impact of increased chest CT utilization on mortality and the risk of overdiagnosis.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Pneumonia</kwd><kwd>Computed tomography</kwd><kwd>Incidence</kwd><kwd>Mortality</kwd><kwd>Overdiagnosis</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro"><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>Pneumonia remains a major global health threat, particularly among older adults [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>]. Annually, pneumonia causes over 1.5 million deaths among individuals aged &#x02265;60 years worldwide [<xref rid="B2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. Considering the aging of the global population, the absolute burden of pneumonia in older adults is expected to increase.</p><p>The demonstration of pulmonary infiltrates by imaging studies is a cornerstone of pneumonia diagnosis, with chest radiography considered the standard tool [<xref rid="B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref><xref rid="B4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref><xref rid="B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. Chest computed tomography (CT) can detect early stage or mild pneumonia that may be missed on standard radiographs [<xref rid="B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref><xref rid="B7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. However, chest CT has limited accessibility, produces more radiation exposure, and is more expensive compared to chest radiography. Therefore, chest CT is generally not considered an initial imaging modality for suspected pneumonia [<xref rid="B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref><xref rid="B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>].</p><p>CT accessibility has significantly improved. The number of CT examinations increased globally from 220 million to 403 million between 2006 and 2018 [<xref rid="B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. In the US, CT utilization among older adults (aged &#x02265;65 years) increased from 204 to 428 per 1,000 person-years between 2000 and 2016 [<xref rid="B10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>]. Increased access to CT may have influenced the diagnosis and management of pneumonia. However, population-level trends in obtaining chest CT in patients with pneumonia remain underexplored. Furthermore, the evolution of the population-level burden of pneumonia associated with changes in the accessibility to chest CT has not been clearly established.</p><p>Therefore, this study aimed to investigate the trends in chest CT utilization among patients with pneumonia and examine its association with pneumonia incidence and mortality in a population-based cohort of older Korean adults.</p></sec><sec sec-type="materials|methods"><title>MATERIALS AND METHODS</title><sec><title>Data and Study Population</title><p>This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB No. E-2306-089-1439), and the requirement for informed consent was waived.</p><p>This population-based cohort study used a subset of the Senior Cohort from the National Health Insurance Service (NHIS) of Korea, which is derived from claims data (<ext-link xlink:href="https://nhiss.nhis.or.kr" ext-link-type="uri">https://nhiss.nhis.or.kr</ext-link>) [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. Since its establishment in 1989, the National Health Insurance has covered approximately 98% of the Korean population, and all healthcare institutions in Korea are designated to provide healthcare services to its beneficiaries. Therefore, NHIS claims data capture nearly the entire spectrum of healthcare utilization in Korea.</p><p>The NHIS-Senior Cohort consists of a random sample representing 8% of the eligible Korean population who met the following criteria: 1) aged 49&#x02013;80 years as of January 1, 2008; and 2) maintained insurance eligibility without death during the calendar year of reaching 60 years of age and in 2008 [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. The database includes demographic characteristics and healthcare utilization records, including clinical diagnoses coded according to the International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems, Tenth Revision (ICD-10), spanning 2002&#x02013;2019.</p><p>For this study, data from individuals aged 61&#x02013;81 years for each calendar year between 2009 and 2018 were analyzed. Individuals aged &#x02264;60 years and data of year 2008 or before were excluded because no deaths occurred in those aged &#x02264;60 years or before 2009. Data from 2019 were also excluded because death information was incomplete for individuals who received healthcare services in late 2019.</p></sec><sec><title>Definition of Variables</title><p>An incident case of pneumonia was defined as the utilization of a medical institution with a primary clinical diagnosis of pneumonia (ICD-10 codes J12&#x02013;18, J69, and J85; <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>), accompanied by a prescription for an antimicrobial drug (234 drugs covered by the National Health Insurance between 2009 and 2018, including antibacterial, antiviral, and antifungal agents). Multiple healthcare visits occurring within 30 days of the initial visit were considered part of a single pneumonia episode.</p><p>Pneumonia incidents were classified into those with or without chest CT, based on the presence of any claim for chest CT acquisition during the pneumonia episode. As surrogate indicators of disease severity, hospitalization and supplemental oxygen therapy were assessed. Episodes were further categorized as hospitalized versus non-hospitalized and with versus without oxygen supplementation. Hospitalization was defined as any inpatient admission during the pneumonia episode, while supplemental oxygen therapy was defined as any claim for oxygen therapy during the pneumonia episode.</p><p>To account for comorbidities potentially influencing pneumonia management and prognosis, seven conditions were considered: cancer, cerebrovascular disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, diabetes, heart disease, liver disease, and utilization of long-term nursing services. The presence of a comorbidity was defined as at least one healthcare utilization with a corresponding diagnosis within 1 year prior to the index date of the pneumonia episode.</p><p>All deaths in Korea are reported to Statistics Korea by law, which maintains a national database on dates and causes of death. Given the limited specificity of cause-of-death information in the Statistics Korea database, any death occurring within 30 days of the pneumonia index date was classified as pneumonia-related mortality, regardless of the recorded cause of death.</p></sec><sec><title>Study Outcomes</title><sec><title>Population-Level Incidence and Mortality Rates of Pneumonia</title><p>To examine temporal trends, age- and sex-standardized incidence rates of pneumonia were calculated for each calendar year from 2009 to 2018. Incidence rates were further stratified by chest CT status (with vs. without) and by severity subtype (hospitalized vs. non-hospitalized; with vs. without oxygen therapy). These analyses were conducted for all pneumonia cases and separately for those with and without chest CT.</p><p>Age- and sex-standardized mortality rates were also estimated annually for 2009&#x02013;2018. Mortality rates were stratified by chest CT status and severity subtype.</p></sec><sec><title>Frequency of Chest CT Acquisition in Patients With Pneumonia</title><p>To assess trends in the frequency of chest CT acquisition in patients with pneumonia, age- and sex-standardized frequencies of chest CT acquisition were estimated annually between 2009 and 2018. Frequencies were further evaluated by severity subtypes and by the presence of comorbidities.</p></sec><sec><title>Frequency of 30-Day Mortality in Patients With Pneumonia</title><p>To evaluate temporal trends in the 30-day mortality rate in patients with pneumonia, age- and sex-standardized 30-day mortality rates were calculated for each year from 2009 to 2018, defined as death within 30 days of the initial pneumonia-related healthcare encounter. Subgroup analyses were conducted by severity subtype and comorbidity status.</p></sec></sec><sec><title>Statistical Analyses</title><p>Age- and sex-standardized incidence and mortality rates of pneumonia were obtained by direct standardization to the mid-year Korean population aged 61&#x02013;81 years in 2024 [<xref rid="B12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>]. Frequencies of chest CT acquisition and 30-day mortality among patients with pneumonia were obtained via direct standardization to the pneumonia patient population in 2018; 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for standardized rates were obtained using the normal approximation to the maximum likelihood estimate [<xref rid="B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>]. Rate ratios between standardized rates of 2009 and 2018 were also estimated [<xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>].</p><p>All statistical analyses were performed using SAS version 8.3 (SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC, USA) and R version 4.3.0 (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>RESULTS</title><sec><title>Population-Level Incidence Rates of Pneumonia</title><p>A total of 930,654 individuals (432,481 males and 498,173 females; mean age &#x000b1; standard deviation: 63 &#x000b1; 8 years as of January 2009) were included in the study (<xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>). Between 2009 and 2018, a total of 187,158 pneumonia episodes were identified. Among the identified pneumonia episodes, hospitalized pneumonia accounted for 25.6% (n = 47,856). Chest CT scans were obtained in 16.9% (31,660) of pneumonia episodes.</p><p>The age- and sex-standardized incidence rate of pneumonia increased from 27.7 per 1,000 person-years in 2009 to 29.4 per 1,000 person-years in 2018 (rate ratio, 1.06 [95% CI, 1.04&#x02013;1.08]; <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>, <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1</xref>). The incidence rate of pneumonia with chest CT gradually increased (3.7 to 5.9 per 1,000 person-years; rate ratio, 1.62 [95% CI, 1.53&#x02013;1.71]), while the incidence rate of pneumonia without chest CT remained stable during the period (24.1 to 23.4 per 1,000 person-years; rate ratio, 0.97 [95% CI, 0.95&#x02013;1.00]). The incidence rates of pneumonia with chest CT gradually increased between 2009 and 2018 for all subtypes of pneumonia (<xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>, <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1</xref>). In contrast, the incidence rates of pneumonia without chest CT gradually declined among hospitalized patients and those receiving supplemental oxygen, while remaining stable in non-hospitalized patients and those not requiring supplemental oxygen (<xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>, <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1</xref>).</p><p><xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table 1</xref> shows the annual incidence rates
of pneumonia from 2009 to 2018.</p></sec><sec><title>Population-Level Mortality Rates of Pneumonia</title><p>From 2009 to 2018, 2,971 mortalities due to pneumonia (death within 30 days from the initial date of pneumonia episode) were identified. Among all pneumonia-related deaths, 84.6% (2,512) occurred in hospitalized patients, whereas 29.6% (878) occurred in patients who underwent chest CT (<xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>).</p><p>The age- and sex-standardized mortality rate of pneumonia decreased from 51.9 per 100,000 person-years in 2009 to 44.2 in 2018 (rate ratio, 0.85 [95% CI, 0.72&#x02013;1.00]). The mortality rate of pneumonia with chest CT gradually increased (11.8 to 14.4 per 100,000 person-years; rate ratio, 1.21 [95% CI, 0.88&#x02013;1.68]), while that of pneumonia without chest CT gradually decreased during the period (40.1 to 29.8 per 100,000 person-years; rate ratio, 0.74 [95% CI, 0.61&#x02013;0.90]) (<xref rid="T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref>, <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2</xref>). Similar tendencies of overall decreasing mortality rates were observed in all subtypes of pneumonia, with a similar increasing mortality rate in pneumonia with chest CT and a decreasing mortality rate in pneumonia without chest CT (<xref rid="T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref>, <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2</xref>).</p><p><xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table 2</xref> shows the annual mortality rates of pneumonia from 2009 to 2018.</p></sec><sec><title>Frequency of Chest CT Acquisition</title><p>The overall crude frequency of chest CT acquisition was 16.9% (31,660/187,158; <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>). The age- and sex-standardized frequency of chest CT acquisition increased from 13.4% to 20.4% (rate ratio, 1.53 [95% CI, 1.44&#x02013;1.61]) between 2009 and 2018 (<xref rid="T5" ref-type="table">Table 5</xref>, <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>). Increasing frequencies of chest CT acquisition were observed in both hospitalized (40.4% to 62.4%; rate ratio, 1.54 [95% CI, 1.45&#x02013;1.64]) and non-hospitalized pneumonia (3.4% to 5.3%; rate ratio, 1.59 [95% CI, 1.40&#x02013;1.81]), and both pneumonia with (46.3% to 64.8%; rate ratio, 1.40 [95% CI, 1.28&#x02013;1.53]) and without (8.2% to 14.2%; rate ratio, 1.74 [95% CI, 1.62&#x02013;1.88]) supplemental oxygen therapy. Similar increasing frequencies of chest CT acquisition were observed, regardless of the presence of comorbidities (<xref rid="T5" ref-type="table">Table 5</xref>, <xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Fig. 1</xref>).</p><p><xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables 3 and 4</xref> show the annual frequency of chest CT acquisition in patients with pneumonia from 2009 to 2018.</p></sec><sec><title>30-Day Mortality Rate of Patients With Pneumonia</title><p>The crude 30-day mortality rate of pneumonia was 1.6% (2,971/187,158; <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>). The age- and sex-standardized 30-day mortality rate decreased from 2.1% to 1.7% (rate ratio, 0.79 [95% CI, 0.67&#x02013;0.93]) between 2009 and 2018 (<xref rid="T6" ref-type="table">Table 6</xref>, <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>). Decreasing 30-day mortality rates were observed in both hospitalized (6.5% to 5.4%; rate ratio, 0.82 [95% CI, 0.69&#x02013;0.98]) and non-hospitalized pneumonia (0.4% to 0.3%; rate ratio, 0.68 [95% CI, 0.43&#x02013;1.08]), and both pneumonia with (11.0% to 9.9%; rate ratio, 0.90 [95% CI, 0.74&#x02013;1.09]) and without (0.7% to 0.5%; rate ratio, 0.75 [95% CI, 0.56&#x02013;1.02]) supplemental oxygen therapy. Similar reductions in 30-day mortality rates were observed regardless of the presence of comorbidities (<xref rid="T6" ref-type="table">Table 6</xref>, <xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Fig. 1</xref>).</p><p><xref rid="S1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables 3 and 4</xref> show the annual 30-day mortality rates of patients with pneumonia from 2009 to 2018.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>This retrospective analysis of the health insurance claims database of older adults in Korea revealed a modest increase in the age- and sex-standardized incidence of pneumonia between 2009 and 2018. The increase was primarily observed in pneumonia with chest CT acquisition. The frequency of chest CT acquisition in patients with pneumonia gradually increased between 2009 and 2018, regardless of severity, as represented by hospitalization, supplemental oxygen therapy, and the presence of comorbidities. In contrast, the population-level age- and sex-standardized mortality rate of pneumonia and the 30-day mortality rate among patients with pneumonia showed a decreasing trend between 2009 and 2018.</p><p>An increasing trend in chest CT utilization has been reported previously [<xref rid="B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref><xref rid="B10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref><xref rid="B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref><xref rid="B16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref><xref rid="B17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref><xref rid="B18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. In this study, the age- and sex-standardized frequencies of chest CT acquisition in older adult patients with pneumonia increased from 13.4% to 20.4%. Various factors may have contributed to this phenomenon, including increased accessibility to CT examination, patient demands for sensitive and accurate diagnosis, and physicians&#x02019; defensive practices. Notably, increasing trends in CT utilization were observed not only in patients with relatively severe pneumonia (hospitalized or requiring supplemental oxygen) or those with comorbidities, but also in patients with relatively mild pneumonia (non-hospitalized or not requiring supplemental oxygen therapy) or those without any comorbidities.</p><p>Although chest CT has higher accuracy than chest radiography for diagnosing pneumonia [<xref rid="B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref><xref rid="B7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref><xref rid="B19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref><xref rid="B20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref><xref rid="B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref><xref rid="B22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>], its use as a first-line examination for diagnosing pneumonia may not be justified. A randomized clinical trial comparing ultra-low-dose CT and chest radiography in patients with suspected acute pulmonary disease in the emergency department reported similar short-term functional health status, mortality rate, hospitalization rate, and length of stay between the two groups [<xref rid="B23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>]. Considering the healthcare costs and radiation exposure related to CT utilization [<xref rid="B24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>], its use may be better reserved as a second-line examination for patients with equivocal chest radiographs, suspected complications of pneumonia, or an immunocompromised status [<xref rid="B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref><xref rid="B8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. Further research is warranted to identify patient subgroups that may benefit from chest CT acquisition and to establish an optimal diagnostic imaging strategy for suspected pneumonia.</p><p>Classically, pneumonia is diagnosed when a patient has symptoms suggestive of acute respiratory infection, and pulmonary infiltrates are confirmed on chest radiography [<xref rid="B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. If a patient&#x02019;s symptoms are equivocal and pulmonary infiltrates are invisible on chest radiography but visible on chest CT, the patient may be diagnosed with pneumonia. Therefore, increased chest CT use could increase the diagnosis of mild pneumonia. In this study, the incidence rate of pneumonia rose modestly from 27.7 to 29.4 per 1,000 person-years between 2009 and 2018. This increase was mainly observed in pneumonia with chest CT (3.7 to 5.9 per 1,000 person-years). The incidence rates of pneumonia with chest CT increased regardless of the relative severity of pneumonia. Notably, the incidence of pneumonia diagnosed without chest CT decreased among patients with relatively severe pneumonia, whereas the rate remained unchanged in those with relatively mild pneumonia. This discrepancy in the incidence rates of relatively mild and severe pneumonia suggests a growing overdiagnosis of mild pneumonia associated with increased chest CT utilization. Growing incidence rates of pneumonia have been reported in multiple studies conducted in various countries [<xref rid="B25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref><xref rid="B26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref><xref rid="B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref><xref rid="B28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref><xref rid="B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>], while its association with chest CT utilization has not been sufficiently investigated. Further investigations are necessary to confirm the association between chest CT utilization and the incidence of pneumonia or the risk of overdiagnosis.</p><p>In this study, the overall burden of death due to pneumonia in the older adult population and the 30-day mortality rate of patients with pneumonia gradually decreased between 2009 and 2018, which is similar to previously reported global data [<xref rid="B2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref><xref rid="B30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref><xref rid="B31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]. The observed increase in pneumonia mortality associated with chest CT may reflect the growing utilization of chest CTs. Several factors, such as improved vaccination, advancements in antimicrobial treatment and other supportive treatments, and improved accessibility to healthcare systems, likely contributed to the reduced mortality burden. Appropriate chest CT acquisition can potentially contribute to clinical decision-making regarding antibiotic administration and hospitalization, and ultimately, patient outcomes [<xref rid="B32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref><xref rid="B33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>]. However, based on the results of this study, determining whether the increased utilization of chest CT contributed to a reduction in mortality is not possible. Further investigations are required to confirm the association between chest CT and mortality risk in patients with pneumonia.</p><p>Notably, the highest incidence and mortality rates were observed in 2012, regardless of relative severity, although the cause could not be identified from the insurance claims data. The cold wave in February 2012 may have partly contributed to the increased burden of pneumonia in older adults [<xref rid="B34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>].</p><p>This study had some limitations. First, the incidence of pneumonia and pneumonia-related mortality were arbitrarily defined for the analysis of insurance claims data; therefore, it may not accurately reflect the exact burden of pneumonia in the general population. Second, although surrogate indicators of pneumonia severity were assessed, the severity of pneumonia could not be fully investigated because the insurance claims data did not include information regarding patients&#x02019; symptoms and signs or laboratory test results. Third, the clinical circumstances for chest CT acquisition (e.g., diagnosis of pneumonia, evaluation of severity, or complications) cannot be determined from the insurance claims data. Fourth, the study covered the period between 2009 and 2018 based on data availability, and trends after 2019 remain unknown. However, the COVID-19 pandemic, which began at the end of 2019, significantly disrupted healthcare resource utilization; hence, excluding this period from the analysis is reasonable even if the data were available. Fifth, outbreaks of certain infectious diseases (e.g., H1N1 Influenza in 2009 and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome in 2015) may have significantly influenced the management of pneumonia. However, separating the temporary influence of outbreaks from long-term trends based on an insurance claims database is difficult. Sixth, potentially relevant metrics such as the frequency of chest radiograph acquisition and regional differences in study outcomes were not investigated. Finally, the generalizability of these findings to populations beyond older adults in Korea remains uncertain.</p><p>In conclusion, chest CT acquisition in older adults with pneumonia increased steadily between 2009 and 2018, regardless of pneumonia severity and comorbidity. During this period, population-level pneumonia incidence increased, mainly in pneumonia with chest CT acquisition. Further investigations are warranted to confirm the influence of increased utilization of chest CT on mortality risk and overdiagnosis in patients with pneumonia.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p><bold>Conflicts of Interest:</bold> The authors have no potential conflicts of interest to disclose.</p></fn><fn fn-type="supported-by"><p><bold>Funding Statement:</bold> None</p></fn></fn-group><sec sec-type="data-availability"><title>Availability of Data and Material</title><p>The datasets generated or analyzed during the study are available in the National Health Insurance Sharing Service (<ext-link xlink:href="https://nhiss.nhis.or.kr" ext-link-type="uri">https://nhiss.nhis.or.kr</ext-link>) on reasonable request.</p></sec><ref-list><ref id="B1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="webpage"><collab>World Health Organization</collab><source>The top 10 causes of death</source><date-in-citation content-type="access-date">accessed on June 26, 2025</date-in-citation><comment>Available at: <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/the-top-10-causes-of-death" ext-link-type="uri">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/the-top-10-causes-of-death</ext-link></comment></element-citation></ref><ref id="B2"><label>2</label><element-citation publication-type="webpage"><collab>Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation</collab><source>GBD (global burden of disease study) results &#x02013; Interactive data visuals</source><date-in-citation content-type="access-date">accessed on June 26, 2025</date-in-citation><comment>Available at: <ext-link xlink:href="https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-results" ext-link-type="uri">https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-results</ext-link></comment></element-citation></ref><ref id="B3"><label>3</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author">
